{"cuisine/tunisian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tunisian-cuisine","n":"Tunisian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Tunisia","intro":[["Tunisian cuisine, the cuisine of Tunisia, consists of the cooking traditions, ingredients, recipes and techniques developed in Tunisia since  antiquity. It is mainly a blend of Arab, Mediterranean, Punic, and Berber cuisine. Historically, Tunisian cuisine witnessed influence and exchanges with many cultures and nations like Italians, Andalusians, French and Arabs."],["Like many countries in the Mediterranean basin, the Tunisian cuisine is heavily based on olive oil, spices, tomatoes, seafood and meat. Yet, it has a distinctive spiciness that differs it from surrounding cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tunisian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunisian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Our Tunisian Table","ref":"site/ourtunisiantable-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ourtunisiantable.com","note":"Tunisian and Maghreb recipes such as lablebi and brik, written by Munya and Rim.","url":"https://ourtunisiantable.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Tunisia Cookbook: Healthy Red Cuisine from Carthage to Kairouan","ref":"cookbook/the-tunisia-cookbook-healthy-red-cuisine-from-carthage-to-kairouan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Haffida Ben Rejeb Latta","note":"English language collection of Tunisian recipes.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Bazin (bread)","dish/bazin-bread"],["Couscous","dish/couscous"],["Mafrum","dish/mafrum"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Zucchini","ingredient/zucchini"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/lower-saxon-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/lower-saxon-cuisine","n":"Lower Saxon cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Germany","intro":[["Lower Saxon cuisine covers a range of regional, North German culinary traditions from the region correspondingly broadly to the state of Lower Saxony, which in many cases are very similar to one another, for example cuisine from the areas of Oldenburg, Brunswick, or East Frisia."],["It is mainly indigenous and in some cases very hearty, with many cultural dishes including poultry, venison, turkey, and vegetables. Many other recipes also include potatoes, asparagus and North Sea fish, all of which are harvested in the region."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"lower-saxon-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Saxon_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/croatian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/croatian-cuisine","n":"Croatian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Croatia","intro":[["Croatian cuisine is heterogeneous and is known as a cuisine of the regions, since every region of Croatia has its own distinct culinary tradition. Its roots date back to ancient times. The differences in the selection of foodstuffs and forms of cooking are most notable between those in mainland and those in coastal regions. Mainland cuisine is more characterized by Slavic features and influences from the more recent contacts with Turkish, Hungarian and Austrian cuisine, using lard for cooking, and spices such as black pepper, paprika, and garlic. The coastal region bears the influences of Greek and Roman cuisine, as well as of the later Mediterranean cuisine, in particular Italian (especially Venetian). Coastal cuisines use olive oil, herbs and spices such as rosemary, sage, bay leaf, oregano, marjoram, cinnamon, clove, nutmeg, and lemon and orange rind. Peasant cooking traditions are based on imaginative variations of several basic ingredients (cereals, dairy products, meat, fish, vegetables, nuts) and cooking procedures (stewing, grilling, roasting, baking), while bourgeois cuisine involves more complicated procedures and use of selected herbs and spices. Charcuterie is part of the Croatian culinary tradition in all regions. Food and recipes from other former Yugoslav countries are also popular in Croatia."],["Croatian cuisine can be divided into several distinct cuisines (Dalmatia, Dubrovnik, Gorski Kotar, Istria, Lika,  Međimurje, Podravina, Slavonija, Zagorje) each of which has specific cooking traditions, characteristic of the area and not necessarily well known in other parts of Croatia. Most dishes, however, can be found all across the country, with local variants."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"croatian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croatian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Da mi je nešto slatko","ref":"site/damijenestoslatko-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"damijenestoslatko.com","note":"Croatian baking and dessert blog with English versions of its recipes.","url":"https://www.damijenestoslatko.com/en/author/anamarija","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Croatia: The Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/croatia-the-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Mate Janković","note":"Large survey of Croatian regional cooking, published by Ljevak.","url":"https://www.ljevak.hr/croatia-the-cookbook","suggested":true},{"name":"Best of Croatian Cooking","ref":"cookbook/best-of-croatian-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Liliana Pavicic, Gordana Pirker-Mosher","note":"Hippocrene collection of classic Croatian dishes.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p328/BestofCroatianCooking.html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Slavonian honey","dish/slavonian-honey"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/note-by-note-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/note-by-note-cuisine","n":"Note by Note cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Molecular gastronomy","cuisine/molecular-gastronomy"]],"sub":"Style of cooking","intro":[["Note by Note cuisine is a style of cooking based on molecular gastronomy, created by French chemist Hervé This. Dishes are made using pure compounds instead of using animal or plant tissues. This said the cuisine is like \"a painter using primary colours, or a musician composing electroacoustic music, wave by wave, using a computer\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Note_by_Note_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Molecular gastronomy","cuisine/molecular-gastronomy"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Molecular gastronomy","cuisine/molecular-gastronomy"]]}],"cont":null},"ingredient/wheat-germ":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/wheat-germ","n":"Wheat germ","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The germ of a cereal grain is the part that develops into a plant;"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-wheat-germ"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheat_germ"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Apple bread","dish/apple-bread"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"]]}]},"ingredient/black-turtle-bean":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/black-turtle-bean","n":"Black turtle bean","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The black turtle bean is a small, shiny variety of the common bean (Phaseolus vulgaris) used in many cuisines. Like all varieties of the common bean, it is native to the Americas, but has been introduced around the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-black-turtle-bean"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_turtle_bean"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"]]],["Latin American cuisine","cuisine/latin-american-cuisine",[["Frijoles negros","dish/frijoles-negros"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Adzuki bean","ingredient/adzuki-bean"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Mung bean","ingredient/mung-bean"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"dish/bebinca":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bebinca","n":"Bebinca","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Goan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bebinca or bebinka, is a layer cake of Indo-Portuguese cuisine in former Estado da Índia Portuguesa, Goa. It’s also referred as Queen of Goan Desserts. In traditional baking, a bebinca has between 7 and 16 layers, but bakeries can modify the cake recipe as per convenience and taste. It is especially popular during the Christmas season, but is available all year round due to tourism in Goa. It is also easily available to carry and preserve for a long time or eaten fresh."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bebinca"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bebinca"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Goan cuisine","cuisine/goan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Goan Food Recipes","ref":"site/goanfoodrecipes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"goanfoodrecipes.com","note":"Recipe blog dedicated to traditional Goan home dishes.","url":"https://www.goanfoodrecipes.com/cuisine/goan","suggested":true}]}]},"ingredient/horse-meat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/horse-meat","n":"Horse meat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["The consumption of horse meat, also known as hippophagy, forms a significant part of the culinary traditions of many countries, particularly in Europe and Asia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-horse-meat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horse_meat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine",[["Bickyburger","dish/bickyburger"],["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"]]],["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine",[["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tarta